WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.425 --> 00:00:02.050
AUDIENCE: One of the
biggest challenges

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.508
in the visual
effects industry when

00:00:03.508 --> 00:00:06.310
you work a feature
movie, everybody

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.550
in the last few months
needs render power.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.280
So in your situation, let's say
you get everybody to go online,

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.540
and then everybody
suddenly needs render power

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.760
these last two, three months.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:16.830
What is your capacity?

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.900
Where are the limits?

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.365
Or is it possible that
end up in a situation

00:00:20.365 --> 00:00:21.490
where it's like, oh, sorry.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.340
You have to wait a
week or so before we

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.100
can give you render nodes?

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.475
JEFF KEMBER: So at this point,
I turn around and tell you

00:00:27.475 --> 00:00:29.450
that we have unlimited
compute power.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:30.950
AUDIENCE: But will you have it?

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.720
Because now, you
don't have the market.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.840
Because I can tell from
first-hand experience, most

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.460
Edge studios, they go
and rent render nodes.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.394
And I had when I went to the
render node company and said,

00:00:42.394 --> 00:00:43.060
what's going on?

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.110
Everybody wants render nodes.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.318
What show are you working on?

00:00:45.318 --> 00:00:47.560
And we were all
working on 2012 movie,

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.520
and we couldn't
get render nodes.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.130
And it got even to the point
where I couldn't buy CPUs

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.230
because all the vendors
were like, no, we

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.310
have to wait two months.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:56.569
All the CPUs are sold out.

00:00:56.569 --> 00:00:58.360
JEFF KEMBER: I'm actually
with you on that.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.660
I worked on a show
where we were in Sydney.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.530
And we ended up having to
get the avatar render farm

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.840
and put in the container and
float it across to Sydney,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.210
and then hook it up at the docks
in order to finish our show.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.710
So I totally
understand the scarcity

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.790
of resource in that regard.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:17.500
What I can say is that Google
buys about 19%, just under 20%,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.310
of the global Intel production.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.450
It's just crazy how
many cores there are.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.130
The fact that we have this
planetary network, and also

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.850
the fact that we have data
centers in multiple locations

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.960
in the planet, we have customers
who are rendering in Iowa,

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.896
for instance, in central
US, who are in Asia

00:01:35.896 --> 00:01:37.770
because it's slightly
less expensive for them

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:38.460
to render there.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.990
And once you get past the
whole file system in the cloud

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:43.395
and syncing your
asset issue, you

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:44.811
can render anywhere
on the planet.

00:01:44.811 --> 00:01:49.450
So we are much less susceptible
to localization issues

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.810
as far as there's a
huge amount of rendering

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.520
required in A or B.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.544
Also, because of our
clients are diversified

00:01:55.544 --> 00:01:56.960
across a whole
bunch of verticals.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.840
So although the visual
effects industry

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.330
is going to have the
summer blockbuster

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.390
and the Thanksgiving push
and all of the little bumps,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.090
or commercial divisions are
going to have the Super Bowl,

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.137
those are quite small
workloads compared

00:02:10.137 --> 00:02:12.220
to the planetary compute
resource that Google has.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:15.880
So although I can't promise
truly unlimited cores,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.540
if you get in touch with
us a few days in advance,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.220
capacity planning can
move things around

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.140
to a significant level.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.200
There's data loads that can
be moved from one data center

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:23.780
to another.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.060
If you need 10,000 cores on
Wednesday and it's Monday,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.084
talk to us, and
concessions can be made.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.966
I will hand the mic
down just in case

00:02:34.966 --> 00:02:37.974
if anyone else wants to hold it.

00:02:37.974 --> 00:02:39.390
MATT O'CONNOR: The
one other thing

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.098
I would add, from a
Google point of view,

00:02:41.098 --> 00:02:46.590
is we would relish the
opportunity to try to break us.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:53.850
As Jeff points out, we have
a lot of cores available.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.410
AUDIENCE: Question back here.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.060
SIMON WISTOW: I was going
to say the other big program

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.060
during render pushes is
that everyone's sleeping

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.540
under the desk and the office
gets really, really dark

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.190
and funky, and everyone smells.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.999
That's the other limiting
factor, lack of swag t-shirts

00:03:07.999 --> 00:03:09.040
that you can change into.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:13.940
AUDIENCE: I guess
one other thing, too,

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.210
for production people,
us in the fields,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.730
if we're in the
jungles of Costa Rica

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.220
or anywhere that maybe we
might not have connectivity

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.880
to the cloud, what
kind of offerings,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.450
or do you guys know of anything,
kind of a queuing system?

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.510
Where hey, I can be
with no connectivity,

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.420
do my job, almost a script-like,
without being a programmer

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.210
myself.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:33.917
Once I get connectivity,
everything's

00:03:33.917 --> 00:03:36.250
going up to the cloud and
computing and everything else.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.730
I don't know if
that makes sense,

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.146
but it's one of
the things that we

00:03:39.146 --> 00:03:41.020
deal with on a
regular basis where

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.010
we don't have connectivity,
like South Africa,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.170
for example, when we've
been there working

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.770
and we want to work
with in the cloud.

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.925
Is there any kind of queueing
model that is coming up,

00:03:50.925 --> 00:03:53.065
I guess, in the
future of offline?

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.940
JEFF KEMBER: I'll pick
that up really quickly

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.240
and say we don't have a
specific offering for the middle

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.700
of Africa on set.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.490
However, if you look at
technologies like the Loon

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.640
project, where we
have big hydrogen

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.120
balloons flying around the
planet providing connectivity.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.370
And we also have an
aerospace division

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:15.520
that we've announced
that has giant flying

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.519
airplanes and other cool
technology like that.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.420
You can Google that
up and find out more.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.470
But the point is the possibility
of providing connectivity

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.110
anywhere on the planet
is in the future.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.680
Today, we are partnering
with companies.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.830
I can suggest Iron
Mountain being local here

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.840
in Los Angeles, where
you can potentially

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.490
take a Hitachi BlueArc
in the back of a truck

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.636
and plug it in with 40-gig
pipes and upload to the cloud

00:04:38.636 --> 00:04:40.260
and not have to burden
your own system.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.360
So happy to talk
with you further

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.820
about possible ideas you have
as far as how to make that go.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.200
But beyond the global wireless
goodness and a pickup truck,

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.303
those are kind of our
two options right now.

00:04:51.303 --> 00:04:52.678
Anyone else want
to pick that up?

00:04:59.305 --> 00:04:59.846
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:04:59.846 --> 00:05:00.520
I'm [INAUDIBLE].

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.255
I'm a storage
engineer at Google.

00:05:02.255 --> 00:05:03.630
So the cool thing
about Google is

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.409
we have a very
large global network

00:05:06.409 --> 00:05:08.700
with more interconnection
points than any other network

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:09.739
provider on the planet.

00:05:09.739 --> 00:05:11.780
And we actually have
connectivity to South Africa

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.280
as well.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.060
That's one of the
peering points.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.320
And so one of the cool things
about the Google Cloud Platform

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.350
is that once your network
packet comes from your facility

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.670
through some ISP, typically
directly to Google,

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.222
then you get a high-speed
first class ticket

00:05:27.222 --> 00:05:28.430
straight to our data centers.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.270
So the reason we run our
global fiber backbone

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.790
is because we want to
control high traffic runs.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.210
It's a software-defined
network that's heavily

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.780
optimized for client traffic.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.440
So there's basically full
utilization with no congestion

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.430
for your traffic.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.064
So as soon as you make it to the
Google connection points, then

00:05:47.064 --> 00:05:49.230
you go straight to our data
centers and our network.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.494
So that's the best we can offer.

00:05:51.494 --> 00:05:53.160
MILO BABIC: One of
the things that we're

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.141
doing for our customers
is offering them

00:05:56.141 --> 00:05:57.640
an additional piece
of software that

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.270
allows them to ingest content
into the cloud environment

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.840
and automatically adjust to
the networking conditions.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.480
To give you an example is if
the average video feed runs

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.280
at six megabits per second
and your bandwidth drops down

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.480
to one megabit per second,
we'll automatically

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.780
additionally compress
that, add a little bit

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.660
of additional latency
on top of that,

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.350
just to make sure that we
can get all the bits in.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.266
We'll reorder the packets
and everything else,

00:06:21.266 --> 00:06:24.220
make sure that they do land
inside a cloud environment,

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.890
until either better
connectivity becomes available

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.810
or there's ability
to peer directly

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:30.810
into the Google network.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:41.410
AUDIENCE: You guys mentioned
about the MPAA certification

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:43.390
as the second half of 2015.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:45.040
How realistic is that?

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.080
Because a small
studio that would

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.640
like to get bigger
projects and count

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.140
with a cloud platform for that.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.720
If the studios don't
allow you to use it,

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.405
that's a big problem because
basically, we cannot do it.

00:06:59.405 --> 00:07:01.280
MATT O'CONNOR: So there's
two pieces to that.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.830
One is right now, we're
waiting on the MPAA.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:12.670
We're waiting for their revised
best practices document.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:18.410
I'm pretty confident that
we can meet or exceed

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.900
the requirements that
are in that document.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:27.380
So my challenge becomes, how do
I convey that to the industry?

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.630
And so this is a
little bit of what

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:33.270
we'd like to help facilitate
with the industry is,

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.210
how do you want to verify this?

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:42.450
Who do you want to trust in
terms of yes, these security

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:46.070
controls at this vendor are
meeting this requirement

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:47.690
in the specification?

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.770
Is it going to be a
self-certified process,

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:57.590
or are we going to use the
big five auditing firms?

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.200
We'd like to help facilitate
that conversation so

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.490
that we can do it in a standard
fashion and it makes sense.

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:13.130
So the realistic timeline on
being able to-- as soon as I

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.830
get my hand on that spec, I
can answer it pretty quickly.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.400
I have all the evidence.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.600
And based upon what I
believe is in there,

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:25.110
I think we have good
answers to those questions.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.750
Whether you believe me or
not, that's another thing.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.190
But I've got no reason to
not tell you the truth.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.530
I mean, I want your business.

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:35.400
I don't want to trick you.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.100
So-- no, no, no,
but I mean, it's-

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.169
AUDIENCE: My concern
is not just Google.

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:41.150
It's the whole
interaction with the MPAA.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:42.909
MATT O'CONNOR: Yeah.

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:49.540
So our hope is that the industry
embraces that as a standard

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:51.780
and it works for them.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.030
Short of that, the other
thing that we're doing

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.950
is we're also engaged
in conversations

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.340
with the major
studios and talking

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:01.740
to them about the unique
requirements they might have

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.180
as a different
company, or how we

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:09.350
can work with them to satisfy
answering their questions.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.080
They may have an
independent auditor

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.450
that they'd like us to
talk to and work with.

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:15.550
We've had some discussions
along those lines.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.120
They may want to do some
auditing themselves.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.560
We're having some discussions
along those lines.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:26.920
So we're trying
to navigate that.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:30.070
And we're trying to
do it as quickly as we

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.790
can so that it's not a barrier.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:37.707
I wish I could tell you, yep,
December 15 we'll be done.

00:09:37.707 --> 00:09:39.540
I don't want to make
that commitment to you.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.620
But what I will commit to is
that this is important to us.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.240
And we are watching
it very, very closely.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.900
And we're doing, I
think, everything

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:55.586
that we can do to make sure that
we're making progress there.

00:09:55.586 --> 00:09:57.960
I think that's probably the
best answer I could give you.

00:10:05.187 --> 00:10:06.270
AUDIENCE: So I'll step in.

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.820
I was part of the team that
helped craft that stuff

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.270
and hand it off to the MPAA.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:12.990
So if you want to get
an advanced look at some

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.580
of that stuff to be ready
for it when it comes out,

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:18.620
I would look at the CSA, the
Cloud Security Alliance, which

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:20.740
helped craft the
document, and ISE,

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:24.880
which is the lead
auditor for Marvel

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.460
and the Disney groups,
which help also drive this.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.960
So those two organizations
would be the best one

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:31.603
to get an advance view.

00:10:31.603 --> 00:10:32.530
AUDIENCE: CSA and ISE?

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:36.004
AUDIENCE: Yes, CSA and ISE.

00:10:36.004 --> 00:10:36.545
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:10:36.545 --> 00:10:37.830
My name's Eric Weaver.

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.300
I work with the ETC.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:42.040
So we help drive some of
those standards and changes.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:43.630
So.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:44.380
MODERATOR: Thanks.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:51.120
Additional questions,
questions, questions?

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.090
I think it's time, then.

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:55.790
Please thank all of our
panelists, all of our speakers.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:56.540
Good job, guys.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.090
[APPLAUSE]

